 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_9jsfFCnBWCnx7QCp",
   "name": "Deriving the Equation for Resistors in Parallel",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Deriving the equation for resistors in parallel"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- In a **parallel **circuit, the **reciprocal **of the **combined resistance** of two or more resistors is the **sum **of the **reciprocal **of the **individual resistances**\n- In a parallel circuit:\n\n  - The **current **is **split **at the junction (and therefore between resistors)\n  - The **potential difference** is the **same **through all resistors\n- The equation for combined resistors in parallel is derived using Kirchhoff’s laws:"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/07/10-1-2-5-derivation-of-resistors-in-parallel-1.png",
   "file": "assets/d5878f8201ed-10-1-2-5-derivation-of-resistors-in-parallel-1.png",
   "alt": "10-1-2-5-derivation-of-resistors-in-parallel-1",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/10/10.1.2.5-Derivation-of-resistors-in-parallel-2.png",
   "file": "assets/5a0a735f9a59-10-1-2-5-derivation-of-resistors-in-parallel-2.png",
   "alt": "Derivation of resistors in parallel (2), downloadable AS & A Level Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_3FdkPXGJ3SjWhMDH",
   "name": "Resistors in Parallel",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Resistors in parallel"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- When two or components are connected in parallel: **The reciprocal of the combined resistance is the sum of the reciprocals of the individual resistances**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Resistors in parallel"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/09/10.1.2.6-Resistors-in-parallel-diagram.png",
   "file": "assets/81a170589725-10-1-2-6-resistors-in-parallel-diagram.png",
   "alt": "Resistors in parallel diagram, downloadable AS & A Level Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***Resistors connected in parallel have the same voltage***"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The equation for the combined resistance, *R*  of resistors in parallel is:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\frac{1}{R}=\\frac{1}{R_{1}}+\\frac{1}{R_{2}}+\\frac{1}{R_{3}}...$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- This means the combined resistance decreases and is less than the resistance of any of the individual components\n- For example, If two resistors of equal resistance are connected in parallel, then the combined resistance will halve"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Maths tip"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The reciprocal of a value is $\\frac{1}{value}$\n- For example, the reciprocal of a whole number such as 2 equals $\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n  - The reciprocal of $\\frac{1}{2}$ is 2\n- If the number is already a fraction, the numerator and denominator are ‘flipped’ round"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Reciprocals"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/09/10.1.2.6-Reciprocals.png",
   "file": "assets/73bd4d8ab353-10-1-2-6-reciprocals.png",
   "alt": "Reciprocals, downloadable AS & A Level Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***The reciprocal of a number is 1 ÷ number***"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- In the case for the resistance R, this becomes $\\frac{1}{R}$. To get the value of R from $\\frac{1}{R}$, you must calculate $\\frac{1}{youranswer}$\n- You can also use the reciprocal button on your calculator (labelled either x<sup>-1</sup> or $\\frac{1}{x}$, depending on your calculator"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "The circuit below shows 3 resistors connected in parallel.\n\nWhich value gives the combined resistance of all the resistors in this circuit?\n\n**A. **$\\frac{5R}{2}$**      **\n\n**B.** $\\frac{2}{5R}$\n\n**C. **$\\frac{5}{2R}$**      **\n\n**D.** $\\frac{2R}{5}$\n\n![10-2-4-we-resistors-in-parallel--cie-new](assets/1d821e9aa46a-10-2-4-we-resistors-in-parallel-cie-new.png)\n\n**Answer:**** ****D**\n\n**Step 1: Resistors in parallel equation**\n\n$\\frac{1}{R}=\\frac{1}{R_{1}}+\\frac{1}{R_{2}}+....=\\frac{1}{R_{1}}+\\frac{1}{R_{2}}+\\frac{1}{R_{3}}$\n\n**Step 2: Substitute in the values given from the equation**\n\n$\\frac{1}{R} _{T} = \\frac{ 1}{R} + \\frac{1}{2 R} + \\frac{ 1}{R} = ( 1 + 1 \\frac{1}{2} + 1 ) \\frac{1}{R} = \\frac{5}{2 R}$\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the total resistance**\n\n$\\frac{1}{R_{T}}=\\frac{5}{2R}$\n\n$R_{T}=\\frac{2R}{5}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "The most common mistake is to leave the answer as 1/R<sub>T</sub>. Remember to calculate $\\frac{1}{answer}$ to get the value of R<sub>T</sub>."
  }
 ]
}